package lockstep:inference@0.1.0;

/// Wraps the [`inference`] interface in a host-only world. The
/// platform's wasm-runtime targets this world with `bindgen!` to
/// generate one canonical Rust module of inference types + a Host
/// trait. Every per-game player bindgen uses a `with:` directive to
/// point at this same module so a single Host impl handles every
/// game's inference calls.
///
/// Guests should NOT target this world — it exists for the HOST. Agents
/// target `lockstep:agent`'s `agent` world, which imports the [`inference`]
/// interface directly (and so needs the Host registered on the linker the
/// platform sets up).
///
/// That used to read "agents implement their game's `player-<role>` world".
/// Those worlds are gone: per-game player WITs died when payloads became
/// opaque bytes, and `world agent` was then split out of `lockstep:engine`
/// into its own package. There is now ONE agent world, shared by every game.
world host {
    import inference;
}

/// Host-provided inference capability.
///
/// The platform implements this in Rust on top of the ONNX runtime;
/// guest WASM components import it but cannot implement it.
///
/// EVERY agent gets it: `lockstep:agent`'s `agent` world imports this
/// unconditionally and the host always installs it on the linker, so there is
/// nothing for an agent to opt into. An agent shipping a hand-written policy
/// simply never calls `infer`. (There is no per-game opt-in any more — games
/// no longer author player WITs of their own.)
///
/// The `artifact` argument is a name declared in the agent bundle's
/// `lockstep.toml` (e.g. `"policy"`); the host resolves it relative to
/// the running agent's bundle. Guests do not see global model IDs —
/// names are scoped to their own bundle.
interface inference {
    use types.{named-tensor, inference-error};

    /// Run inference on the named artifact.
    ///
    /// The host:
    ///   1. resolves `artifact` against the current agent's bundle;
    ///   2. validates input tensor shapes / dtypes against the loaded
    ///      model's declared signature;
    ///   3. enforces per-tick + per-call budgets (see `docs/resources.md`);
    ///   4. invokes ORT and returns the model's outputs as a list of
    ///      named tensors.
    ///
    /// On any failure the call returns `result::err(inference-error)`
    /// rather than trapping; the guest may fall back to a hand-coded
    /// policy.
    infer: func(
        artifact: string,
        inputs: list<named-tensor>,
    ) -> result<list<named-tensor>, inference-error>;
}
